WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.200
An Activity diagram explains how work flows through
actions and control structures.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:13.845
It is useful when the important question is what happens
next, under which condition, and which work can proceed in
parallel.

00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:20.515
Our request-intake example validates input, stores valid
work and notifies the people involved.

00:00:20.635 --> 00:00:29.010
We will add an action, inspect decisions and partitions,
compare a loop with a branch, and review supported
connection and ordering controls.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.680
Keep each path traceable from start to outcome.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.100
Use Insert and Action to add Record audit event.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:46.745
An action label should describe something the process
does, rather than a vague status or a screen title.

00:00:46.865 --> 00:00:51.015
Inspect where the action is inserted and check the source
order.

00:00:51.135 --> 00:00:56.385
Creating another box is not enough if the surrounding flow
no longer makes sense.

00:00:56.505 --> 00:01:04.080
Use the supported ordering or connection workflow to place
it appropriately, and review the path on both sides of the
new action.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.975
Select Receive request.

00:01:10.095 --> 00:01:15.070
Review its editable label and presentation properties in
the inspector.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:20.915
A color or stereotype can classify an action, while a note
explains an assumption.

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:24.510
Keep the label focused on the work being performed.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:31.855
Renaming an action should improve the explanation without
silently changing which branch or partition owns it.

00:01:31.975 --> 00:01:38.475
After editing, inspect the source and the surrounding flow
rather than checking only the selected box.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.675
The validation decision has a successful path and a
correction path.

00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:51.745
Read the condition and branch labels, then trace each path
to its outcome.

00:01:51.865 --> 00:01:56.415
A decision is not simply a decorative diamond between two
actions.

00:01:56.535 --> 00:02:00.710
The condition must explain why the process chooses one
path.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:09.205
Open Flow structure to inspect the supported constructs,
and use the source to check how a structure begins,
changes branch and ends.

00:02:09.325 --> 00:02:13.800
Keep conditions and labels concise enough to follow on the
diagram.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.925
The extended example repeats receipt and validation while
corrections are needed.

00:02:23.045 --> 00:02:26.720
Compare the loop's condition with a one-time decision.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:36.090
Repetition should have an understandable continuation
condition and an exit; otherwise the diagram leaves the
reader guessing when work can proceed.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.835
Different loop constructs express different orderings of
the test and the body.

00:02:40.955 --> 00:02:47.930
Use a small reference example to check the syntax before
nesting several structures into one large process.

00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:57.958
After storing a request, confirmation and support
notification can proceed in parallel in this model.

00:02:58.078 --> 00:03:06.003
Fork and join structure makes that distinction explicit;
two boxes on the same horizontal level are not enough by
themselves.

00:03:06.123 --> 00:03:10.498
The Request processing partition groups work by
responsibility.

00:03:10.618 --> 00:03:15.643
A partition is different from a branch condition or a
scheduling dependency.

00:03:15.763 --> 00:03:21.438
Explain those meanings separately and check where the flow
enters and leaves the grouped work.

00:03:24.967 --> 00:03:30.492
Use the diagram's supported connection and reordering
tools to organize the workflow.

00:03:30.612 --> 00:03:37.612
Start and terminal constructs explain entry and outcome,
while arrows make the flow explicit where supported.

00:03:37.732 --> 00:03:45.732
Select an object before choosing a connection handle, and
inspect the resulting source rather than assuming the drop
target was correct.

00:03:45.852 --> 00:03:54.677
For specialized valid Plant UML constructs without a
dedicated visual operation, use source editing and inspect
the rendered result.

00:03:54.797 --> 00:04:01.772
The goal is an unambiguous process, not a promise that
every syntax variation has a separate button.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.475
Open the flow arrow tool and choose Receive request as its
insertion point.

00:04:10.595 --> 00:04:16.870
Label the flow Validated input and choose a dashed line so
its authored style is explicit.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.865
Add the arrow, then inspect its statement and current
preview.

00:04:20.985 --> 00:04:26.735
The insertion point determines where this flow annotation
sits in the activity structure.

00:04:26.855 --> 00:04:33.830
Check that both decision branches still lead to the
intended outcomes before reorganizing a larger workflow.

00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:42.358
Select Receive request and Store request together and
inspect the bulk panel.

00:04:42.478 --> 00:04:49.528
Apply a supported shared style and verify that it changes
presentation without changing the control flow.

00:04:49.648 --> 00:04:54.398
A selected group is not automatically a parallel block or
a partition.

00:04:54.518 --> 00:04:58.493
Use the appropriate structural construct for those
meanings.

00:04:58.613 --> 00:05:07.913
Copy and duplicate only a deliberate selection, then check
how the resulting actions fit into the workflow and
whether their labels remain distinct.

00:05:11.433 --> 00:05:15.033
Pause and add an audit action to the intake model.

00:05:15.153 --> 00:05:24.603
Explain both validation outcomes, then use the extended
example to show how corrections repeat and how two
notifications can proceed in parallel.

00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:30.148
Put related work in a meaningful partition, add a note and
inspect a connection.

00:05:30.268 --> 00:05:33.543
Finally, style a selected pair of actions.

00:05:33.663 --> 00:05:43.963
You have succeeded when every path has an understandable
next step and outcome, and a reader can distinguish
responsibility, branching, repetition and parallelism.

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:54.592
Activity diagrams combine actions with decisions,
repetition, parallel work and responsibility grouping.

00:05:54.712 --> 00:06:00.737
Use each construct for its meaning, and check the
surrounding flow after moving or adding work.

00:06:00.857 --> 00:06:04.932
Keep source available for precision and specialized
syntax.

00:06:05.052 --> 00:06:16.952
The following lessons return to the common editor tools,
review and document workflows, so you can maintain these
models as they evolve instead of treating them as pictures
that must be rebuilt from scratch.
